in": 12.0, "sigma_race": 5.0, "sigma_total": 5.44, "baseline_margin": 9.65, "analyst_adjustment": -0.15, "key_factors": ["Klobuchar's large polling lead and name recognition", "Massive fundraising edge over Demuth", "Minnesota's Democratic-leaning electorate", "Demuth PAC money and a tightening R-sponsored poll"], "rationale": "Polls since June show Klobuchar ahead by 5 to 13 points, averaging around 9, and she has outraised Demuth by roughly 20-to-1. Markets price her near 95%, and raters call the race Likely or Solid D, so a high-single-digit Democratic margin with modest uncertainty fits the evidence.", "watch": "The Oct. 21 debate and the $10 million Demuth PAC infusion could narrow the race if Klobuchar stumbles or the Somalia money-transfer issue gains traction.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Tim Walz (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Minnesota's open governor's race pits Senator Amy Klobuchar against state House Republican leader Lisa Demuth after Tim Walz chose not to run again. Klobuchar enters as a heavy favorite: recent polls show her leading by margins ranging from two to thirteen points, with the most recent surveys clustering around a high-single-digit advantage. She has also built an enormous financial edge, raising over $7 million to Demuth's roughly $360,000, though a Demuth-aligned PAC received a $10 million boost and outside spending has made this the most expensive governor's race in state history.\n\nPrediction markets put Klobuchar's chances around 95%, and most race raters call the contest Likely D, with two rating it Solid D. Demuth, who emerged as a consensus choice to lead Minnesota Republicans, has worked to keep the focus on the economy and business climate. The race remains competitive enough that debates and outside money could shift the margin, but the fundamentals still favor Democrats in a state that has leaned their way in recent cycles.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9683, "margin": 10.03}